<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul">
<title>Dot product versus scalar multiplication</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case:
	<ol>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul-first-dot-prod">
			Compute <m> \udotprod{u}{v} </m>,
			then use that result to compute <m> k (\udotprod{u}{v}) </m>.
		</li>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul-first-smul-left">
			Compute <m> k \uvec{u} </m>,
			then use that result to compute <m> \dotprod{(k \uvec{u})}{\uvec{v}} </m>.
		</li>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul-first-smul-right">
			Compute <m> k \uvec{v} </m>,
			then use that result to compute <m> \dotprod{\uvec{u}}{(k \uvec{v})} </m>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Compare your results in
			<xref
			  text="type-local"
			  first="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul-first-dot-prod"
			  last="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul-first-smul-right"
			>parts</xref>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Reflect on the meaning of the different multiplication operations involved in each of
			<xref
			  text="type-local"
			  first="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul-first-dot-prod"
			  last="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-dot-prod-vs-smul-first-smul-right"
			>parts</xref>.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> k = 6 </m>,
		<m> \uvec{u} = ( 4,  5) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = ( 5, -2) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			<p><m>
				\udotprod{u}{v}
				= 4 \cdot 5 + 5 \cdot (-2)
				= 10
			</m></p>
			<p><m> k (\udotprod{u}{v}) = 60 </m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{u} = (24, 30) </m></p>
			<p><m> \dotprod{(k \uvec{u})}{\uvec{v}} = 24 \cdot 5 + 30 \cdot (-2) = 60 </m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{v} = (30, -12) </m></p>
			<p><m> \dotprod{\uvec{u}}{(k \uvec{v})} = 4 \cdot 30 + 5 \cdot (-12) = 60 </m></p>
		</li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> k = -5 </m>,
		<m> \uvec{u} = ( 0, -4, 1) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = ( 2,  4, 5) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			<p><m>
				\udotprod{u}{v}
				= 0 \cdot 2 + (-4) \cdot 4 + 1 \cdot 5
				= -11
			</m></p>
			<p><m> k (\udotprod{u}{v}) = 55 </m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{u} = (0, 20, -5) </m></p>
			<p><m> \dotprod{(k \uvec{u})}{\uvec{v}} = 0 \cdot 2 + 20 \cdot 4 + (-5) \cdot 5 = 55 </m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{v} = (-10, -20, -25) </m></p>
			<p><m> \dotprod{\uvec{u}}{(k \uvec{v})} = 0 \cdot (-10) + (-4) \cdot (-20) + 1 \cdot (-25) = 55 </m></p>
		</li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> k = 2 </m>,
		<m> \uvec{u} = (-5, -4, 6, 6) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = ( 1,  6, 6, 3) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			<p><m>
				\udotprod{u}{v}
				= (-5) \cdot 1 + (-4) \cdot 6 + 6 \cdot 6 + 6 \cdot 3
				= 25
			</m></p>
			<p><m> k (\udotprod{u}{v}) = 50 </m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{u} = (-10, -8, 12, 12) </m></p>
			<p><m> \dotprod{(k \uvec{u})}{\uvec{v}} = (-10) \cdot 1 + (-8) \cdot 6 + 12 \cdot 6 + 12 \cdot 3 = 50 </m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{v} = (2, 12, 12, 6) </m></p>
			<p><m> \dotprod{\uvec{u}}{(k \uvec{v})} = (-5) \cdot 2 + (-4) \cdot 12 + 6 \cdot 12 + 6 \cdot 6 = 50 </m></p>
		</li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
